# Party Jukebox settings.
#
# The Party Jukebox app keeps this file at
#   ~/Library/Application Support/Party Jukebox/jukebox.conf
# and its Settings window (the note in the menu bar > Settings...) reads and
# writes it for you. You never need to edit it by hand -- but you can: it is
# read by the shell when the jukebox starts, so anything the window does not
# show can go here. Restart the jukebox after editing (menu > Restart Jukebox).
#
# Running from a clone instead of the app, put it in the repo root and run:
#   set -a; . ./jukebox.conf; set +a; node server.js

# A Mac with two network adapters may advertise the wrong one. The easy fix
# is Guest Network in the menu (or Settings > Network): pick the adapter.
# That choice wins over this line and follows the adapter if its address
# changes. To pin an exact address instead:
# PUBLIC_URL="http://192.168.1.40:8484/"

# The neon marquee text.
# JUKEBOX_TITLE="JUKEBOX"

# The playlist the queue mirrors into.
# JUKEBOX_PLAYLIST="Jukebox"

# --- Guest rules -----------------------------------------------------------
# How many songs one phone may have WAITING at once. A song that has started
# playing has left the queue and freed its slot.
# JUKEBOX_MAX_PER_PHONE=25

# The same, for the laptop. The laptop only counts as the laptop when the
# browser is at http://localhost:8484/ -- open it by its LAN address and the
# server sees an ordinary guest and applies the phone limit.
# JUKEBOX_MAX_PER_LAPTOP=25

# Total songs that may be queued by everyone.
# JUKEBOX_MAX_QUEUE=60

# Minutes before the same song may be picked again. 0 turns the rule off.
# The laptop is always exempt from it.
# JUKEBOX_REPEAT_MINUTES=60

# --- Wi-Fi QR code -----------------------------------------------------------
# A second QR code on the laptop screen, above the jukebox one: scan it and
# the phone joins the Wi-Fi. Leave the name unset and it does not appear.
#
# It MUST be the network the laptop is on. A separate guest network usually
# stops phones reaching the laptop, and then the jukebox page will not load.
#
# Use SINGLE quotes. This file is read by the shell, so in double quotes a
# $ or ` in the password is mangled. A ' in the password is written '\''.
# The password is printed under the code too, for phones that will not join
# from a scan -- the code holds it in plain text anyway.
# JUKEBOX_WIFI_NAME='Home Wi-Fi'
# JUKEBOX_WIFI_PASSWORD='the password'
